# Tools: Highlighting a face selection in the plotter

This example shows how to use ``FaceSelection`` together with ``GeometryPlotter``
to visually highlight a filtered subset of faces on top of a 3D geometry.

A bracket model is loaded, a chain of filters is applied to isolate small
triangular/quadrilateral fillet faces in a specific spatial region, and the
result is rendered with the ``highlight`` parameter of
:meth:`GeometryPlotter.plot() <ansys.geometry.core.plotting.plotter.GeometryPlotter.plot>`.

The ``SelectionBuilder`` instance is accessible through ``modeler.create_selection_builder()``.
It should not be instantiated directly by the user.

+++

## Perform required imports

Perform the required imports.

```{code-cell} ipython3
from pathlib import Path
import requests

from ansys.geometry.core import launch_modeler
from ansys.geometry.core.misc.options import ImportOptions
from ansys.geometry.core.plotting import GeometryPlotter
from ansys.geometry.core.selection_builder.selection_builder import RangeType
```

## Download the bracket file

```{code-cell} ipython3
BASE_URL = (
    "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ansys/pyansys-geometry/main/tests/integration/files/"
)


def download_file(filename):
    """Download a file from the PyAnsys Geometry repository."""
    url = BASE_URL + filename
    local_path = Path.cwd() / filename
    response = requests.get(url)
    response.raise_for_status()
    local_path.write_bytes(response.content)
    print(f"Downloaded: {filename}")
    return local_path


bracket_file = download_file("Bracket_Static_Stress.dsco")
```

## Initialize the modeler

```{code-cell} ipython3
modeler = launch_modeler()
print(modeler)
```

## Open the bracket file

```{code-cell} ipython3
options = ImportOptions()
options.import_names = True
design = modeler.open_file(file_path=bracket_file, import_options=options)
design.plot()
```

## Build the face selection

Apply a chain of filters to isolate small fillet faces (3–4 edges, limited
area) that lie within a specific X/Y spatial window.

```{code-cell} ipython3
sb = modeler.create_selection_builder()

all_faces = sb.faces.get_all_faces()
print("All faces:", len(all_faces.items))

faces_3_4_edges = all_faces.filter_faces_by_edge_count(3, 4)
print("Faces with 3-4 edges:", len(faces_3_4_edges.items))

faces_by_area = faces_3_4_edges.filter_faces_by_area(0.00000247858, 0.0002564094)
print("Faces in area range:", len(faces_by_area.items))

faces_by_x = faces_by_area & faces_by_area.get_faces_with_x_location(
    range_type=RangeType.RANGETYPE_CONTAIN, min=0, max=0.010
)
print("Faces in X window:", len(faces_by_x.items))

remove_face = faces_by_x.get_faces_with_y_location(
    range_type=RangeType.RANGETYPE_INTERSECT, min=0.11753, max=0.122
)
print("Faces to exclude:", len(remove_face.items))

final_faces = faces_by_x - remove_face
print("Final highlighted faces:", len(final_faces.items))
```

## Plot with highlight

Pass the ``FaceSelection`` to the ``highlight`` parameter. The selected faces
are rendered on top of the full geometry with a distinct color. Use
``highlight_color`` to customize the color (default is ``"#FF0000"``).

```{code-cell} ipython3
plotter = GeometryPlotter()
plotter.plot(design, highlight=final_faces)
plotter.show()
```

## Close the modeler

```{code-cell} ipython3
modeler.close()
```
